 course('E410','Phonetics and Phonology','Phonetics & Phonology','Speech has a physical form and a patterned organisation. Begin with phonetic description and phonological contrast, then build syllables, word stress and connected speech. Intonation and transcription connect the sound system with meaning in actual interaction.',['Describe speech physically: articulation, sound signals and perception.','Identify contrasts and patterns: phonemes, features, vowels and consonants.','Build larger units: syllables, stress, tone and intonation.','Explain an actual utterance: reduction, assimilation, linking and IPA.'],g410,r410,{'Phonetics','Phonology','Chunking speech','Phone and phoneme','Minimal pairs','Allophones','Generative phonology','Vowel classification','Monophthongs','Diphthongs','Triphthong sequences','Consonant classification','Onset, nucleus, coda and rhyme','Phonotactics','Strong and weak forms','Elision','Regressive assimilation','Stress and prominence','Intonation and its functions','Connected-speech transcription'},{'Phonology','Complementary distribution','Triphthong sequences','Stress placement and suffixes','Types of tone','Tag questions and interpretation','Connected-speech transcription'},[('allophony','Essentials of Linguistics 2: distribution and phonetic similarity','https://openlibrary-repo.ecampusontario.ca/jspui/bitstream/123456789/1489/3/Essentials-of-Linguistics-2nd-edition.pdf'),('triphthongs','University of Ss. Cyril and Methodius: Triphthongs','https://e-ucebnice.ff.ucm.sk/index.php/phonetics-and-phonology-6-5/')])
